Third-Party Data Attribution
============================

This project's CSV/EVTX detection corpus is built on third-party data.

Attack corpus (the 271 malicious .evtx and the CSVs derived from them)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Source : mdecrevoisier — "EVTX-to-MITRE-Attack"
URL    : https://github.com/mdecrevoisier/EVTX-to-MITRE-Attack
License: Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal (CC0 1.0) — public domain dedication

The upstream README and LICENSE ship under `data/evtx/`. In `metadata.json`
these files are marked `source: folder_structure`. The binary .evtx were
transformed to the scored CSV corpus with EvtxECmd (Eric Zimmerman's EZ Tools,
https://ericzimmerman.github.io/). CC0 imposes no legal attribution requirement;
this notice is provided as a matter of academic and ethical practice.

Benign corpus (the .csv under `data/csv/_benign`)
----------------------------------------------------------------
Source : NextronSystems — "evtx-baseline" (goodware Windows event logs)
URL    : https://github.com/NextronSystems/evtx-baseline
License: Apache License 2.0

The benign samples are REAL Windows baseline activity (admin process execution,
account/group changes, logons, PowerShell, registry, file ops) drawn from the
evtx-baseline `.evtx`, transformed to CSV with EvtxECmd and sliced into single-
technique benign near-misses. In `metadata.json` they are marked
`source: evtx-baseline`. The upstream Apache-2.0 license text ships under
`data/evtx/evtx-baseline-LICENSE`.

(2026-06-12: the corpus was reset to be 100% real — the prior hand-authored
synthetic benign controls were removed.)

